::PC_U06_L03_Q01::
Question ID: PC_U06_L03_Q01
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.SEQ.3
Question:
What is an arithmetic series? Use the same lesson method.
{
=A. The sum of terms from an arithmetic sequence#Correct. A series adds terms, and arithmetic means the terms come from an arithmetic sequence.
~B. A list of terms that multiply by the same ratio#Incorrect. Multiplying by the same ratio describes a geometric sequence.
~C. Only the first term of a sequence#Incorrect. A series includes a sum, not only one term.
~D. The graph of an arithmetic pattern#Incorrect. A graph is a representation, not the definition of a series.
}
::PC_U06_L03_Q02::
Question ID: PC_U06_L03_Q02
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.SEQ.3
Question:
Find 5 + 9 + 13 + 17 + 21. Use the same lesson method.
{
~A. 26#Incorrect. 26 is only the first plus last, not the full sum.
~B. 105#Incorrect. This multiplies by too many terms or does not divide by 2.
~C. 45#Incorrect. This leaves out some terms in the sum.
=D. 65#Correct. There are 5 terms and the average of 5 and 21 is 13, so the sum is 65.
}
::PC_U06_L03_Q03::
Question ID: PC_U06_L03_Q03
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.SEQ.3
Question:
Which formula gives the sum of a finite arithmetic series? Use the same lesson method.
{
~A. a_n = a_1 r^(n - 1)#Incorrect. This is a geometric sequence formula, not an arithmetic series formula.
~B. S_n = a_1 + a_n#Incorrect. This adds only two terms and ignores n.
=C. S_n = n(a_1 + a_n) / 2#Correct. This formula uses the number of terms and the average of the first and last terms.
~D. a_n = a_1 + (n - 1)d#Incorrect. This finds one arithmetic term, not the sum of a series.
}
::PC_U06_L03_Q04::
Question ID: PC_U06_L03_Q04
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.SEQ.3
Question:
For 3, 7, 11, ..., what is the 12th term needed before finding S_12? Use the same lesson method.
{
~A. 300#Incorrect. 300 is the sum, not the last term.
=B. 47#Correct. 3 + (12 - 1)4 = 47.
~C. 44#Incorrect. This uses 10 jumps instead of 11.
~D. 50#Incorrect. This is the first plus last total in the series formula, not the 12th term.
}
::PC_U06_L03_Q05::
Question ID: PC_U06_L03_Q05
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.SEQ.3
Question:
Why does the arithmetic series formula divide by 2? Use the same lesson method.
{
=A. It uses the average of the first and last terms#Correct. The formula multiplies n by the average of the first and last terms.
~B. Every arithmetic series has only two terms#Incorrect. Arithmetic series can have any finite number of terms.
~C. The common difference must be halved#Incorrect. The formula averages term values, not the common difference.
~D. It changes a series into a geometric sequence#Incorrect. Dividing by 2 does not change the sequence type.
}
